<!--
  ICS Watch Dog - Jump Host / Bastion Host Sysmon Configuration
  Version:      v1.1
  Author:       ICS Watch Dog Project (https://icswatchdog.com)
  Project:      https://github.com/cutaway-security/ICSWatchDog
  License:      Creative Commons Attribution 4.0

  Minimum Sysmon:   v15+ (schema 4.90)
  Target:           Jump hosts, bastion hosts, kiosk systems used for OT remote access

  SANS ICS 5 Critical Controls Supported:
    #1 ICS Incident Response - Comprehensive forensic evidence on the primary entry point
    #2 Defensible Architecture - Validates jump host is functioning as intended chokepoint
    #3 ICS Network Visibility - Every connection through the jump host is logged with process context
    #4 Secure Remote Access - PRIMARY FOCUS: detects all remote access tool usage, RDP sessions, clipboard activity
    #5 Vulnerability Management - Software execution and change tracking

  CIS Benchmark Alignment:
    Complements CIS Microsoft Windows Server 2019/2022 Benchmark (L2)
    Jump hosts should be hardened to at least CIS L2 with additional restrictions.
    Extends Section 17 (Advanced Audit Policy) with process-level telemetry.

  MITRE ATT&CK Coverage:
    T1021     - Remote Services (RDP, SSH, VNC monitoring)
    T1219     - Remote Access Software (RMM tool detection)
    T1115     - Clipboard Data (Event ID 24 clipboard monitoring)
    T1055.012 - Process Hollowing (Event ID 25 process tampering)
    T1547     - Boot or Logon Autostart Execution (registry monitoring)
    T1490     - Inhibit System Recovery (shadow copy deletion, bcdedit, wbadmin)
    T1562.001 - Disable or Modify Tools (Defender tampering, Sysmon tampering)

  DESCRIPTION:
    Jump hosts and bastion hosts are the primary controlled entry point into OT networks.
    They are high-value targets for attackers and warrant the most comprehensive monitoring
    of any system in the environment. This config is intentionally verbose because:
    - Jump hosts should have VERY predictable process activity
    - Every connection through a jump host is significant
    - Any unexpected software execution warrants investigation
    - Clipboard monitoring detects data exfiltration between networks

    This config enables Event IDs that are disabled in the baseline configs:
    - Event ID 24 (ClipboardChange) - detects data transfer via clipboard
    - Event ID 29 (FileExecutableDetected) - detects new executable files (schema 4.90)

    All include rules use the ATT&CK structured tagging convention.
    See https://icswatchdog.com/attack-tagging/ for the full specification.

    IMPORTANT: This config uses schema 4.90 (Sysmon v15+). Jump hosts should run
    current Windows versions and can support newer Sysmon features.

  TUNING:
    - This config generates MORE events than baseline configs by design
    - Review and exclude your organization's approved remote access solution
    - AppLocker or similar application whitelisting is strongly recommended on jump hosts
    - Monitor event volume during initial deployment and adjust as needed
    - Test in a non-production environment before deploying to production

  DISCLAIMER:
    This configuration is provided as-is for educational and operational use.
    It is NOT tested against all environments and may require tuning for your
    specific systems. Cutaway Security, LLC and contributors assume no liability
    for any impact resulting from the use of this configuration. Users are
    responsible for testing in their own environments before production deployment.

  REFERENCES:
    - SANS ICS 5 Critical Controls: https://www.sans.org/white-papers/five-ics-cybersecurity-critical-controls
    - CISA RMM Guidance: https://www.cisa.gov/news-events/cybersecurity-advisories/aa23-025a
    - CISA Secure Remote Access Guide: https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/2023-06/Guide%20to%20Securing%20Remote%20Access%20Software_clean%20Final_508c.pdf
    - LOLRMM Project: https://lolrmm.io/
    - CIS Benchmarks: https://www.cisecurity.org/benchmark/microsoft_windows_server
    - CISA Ransomware Guide: https://www.cisa.gov/stopransomware/ransomware-guide
-->

<Sysmon schemaversion="4.90">
  <HashAlgorithms>md5,sha256,IMPHASH</HashAlgorithms>
  <!-- CheckRevocation: checks loaded driver code-signing certificate revocation status.
       Requires network access to CRL distribution points / OCSP responders.
       Set to False if this system has no internet or restricted network access. -->
  <CheckRevocation>True</CheckRevocation>

  <EventFiltering>

    <!--
    ================================================================
    EVENT ID 1: PROCESS CREATION [ProcessCreate]
    MINIMAL exclusions. Jump hosts should have very predictable processes.
    Any unexpected process execution warrants investigation.
    ================================================================
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="ProcessCreate" groupRelation="or">
      <ProcessCreate onmatch="exclude">
        <!-- Only the absolute minimum system noise -->
        <Image name="exclude=conhost system noise" condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\conhost.exe</Image>
        <Image name="exclude=Windows Error Reporting" condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\WerFault.exe</Image>
        <Image name="exclude=Windows Error Reporting (32-bit)" condition="is">C:\Windows\SysWOW64\WerFault.exe</Image>
        <Image name="exclude=Windows Error Reporting manager" condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\wermgr.exe</Image>
        <!--
        NOTE: Intentionally fewer exclusions than baseline configs.
        On a jump host, you WANT to see RuntimeBroker, backgroundTaskHost,
        audiodg, etc. if they run - these indicate the system may have
        unnecessary features enabled.
        -->
      </ProcessCreate>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!--
      T1490 Inhibit System Recovery + T1562.001 Disable or Modify Tools
      Ransomware indicator detection: recovery inhibition and security tool disablement.
      These commands are rarely run by legitimate users on a jump host. Investigate every match.
      References:
        https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1490/
        https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1562/001/
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="ProcessCreate-RansomwareIndicators" groupRelation="or">
      <ProcessCreate onmatch="include">
        <!-- Recovery inhibition (T1490) -->
        <CommandLine name="technique_id=T1490,technique=Inhibit System Recovery,detection=Ransomware shadow copy deletion via vssadmin" condition="contains">delete shadows</CommandLine>
        <CommandLine name="technique_id=T1490,technique=Inhibit System Recovery,detection=Ransomware shadow copy deletion via WMI" condition="contains">shadowcopy delete</CommandLine>
        <CommandLine name="technique_id=T1490,technique=Inhibit System Recovery,detection=Ransomware Windows recovery disabled via bcdedit" condition="contains">recoveryenabled no</CommandLine>
        <CommandLine name="technique_id=T1490,technique=Inhibit System Recovery,detection=Ransomware boot status policy bypass via bcdedit" condition="contains">bootstatuspolicy ignoreallfailures</CommandLine>
        <CommandLine name="technique_id=T1490,technique=Inhibit System Recovery,detection=Ransomware Windows backup catalog deletion via wbadmin" condition="contains">delete catalog</CommandLine>
        <CommandLine name="technique_id=T1490,technique=Inhibit System Recovery,detection=Ransomware safe mode boot configuration" condition="contains">bcdedit /set safeboot</CommandLine>
        <!-- Defender/security tool disablement (T1562.001) -->
        <CommandLine name="technique_id=T1562.001,technique=Disable or Modify Tools,detection=Ransomware Defender realtime monitoring disabled" condition="contains">DisableRealtimeMonitoring $true</CommandLine>
        <CommandLine name="technique_id=T1562.001,technique=Disable or Modify Tools,detection=Ransomware Defender path exclusion added" condition="contains">ExclusionPath</CommandLine>
      </ProcessCreate>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!--
    ================================================================
    EVENT ID 2: FILE CREATION TIME CHANGED [FileCreateTime]
    Log all timestomping attempts. Minimal exclusions on jump host.
    MITRE ATT&CK: T1070.006 (Timestomp)
    ================================================================
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="FileCreateTime" groupRelation="or">
      <FileCreateTime onmatch="exclude">
        <Image name="exclude=svchost timestamp updates" condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\svchost.exe</Image>
      </FileCreateTime>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!--
    ================================================================
    EVENT ID 3: NETWORK CONNECTION [NetworkConnect]
    LOG ALL network connections. Jump hosts are chokepoints - every
    connection is significant. NO exclusions.
    ================================================================
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="NetworkConnect" groupRelation="or">
      <NetworkConnect onmatch="exclude"/>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!--
    ================================================================
    EVENT ID 5: PROCESS TERMINATED [ProcessTerminate]
    Log all process termination - useful for session tracking.
    ================================================================
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="ProcessTerminate" groupRelation="or">
      <ProcessTerminate onmatch="exclude">
        <Image name="exclude=conhost termination noise" condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\conhost.exe</Image>
      </ProcessTerminate>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!--
    ================================================================
    EVENT ID 6: DRIVER LOADED [DriverLoad]
    Log all non-Microsoft drivers. Jump hosts should have minimal drivers.
    ================================================================
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="DriverLoad" groupRelation="or">
      <DriverLoad onmatch="exclude">
        <Signature name="exclude=Microsoft signed driver" condition="contains">Microsoft</Signature>
        <Signature name="exclude=Windows signed driver" condition="contains">Windows</Signature>
      </DriverLoad>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!--
    ================================================================
    EVENT ID 7: IMAGE LOADED [ImageLoad]
    ENABLED on jump hosts - monitors DLL loading.
    Jump hosts have predictable DLL activity. Unexpected DLLs warrant
    investigation. This generates significant volume but is warranted
    on high-value chokepoints.
    Exclude known system DLLs to manage volume.
    ================================================================
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="ImageLoad" groupRelation="or">
      <ImageLoad onmatch="exclude">
        <ImageLoaded name="exclude=System32 DLL path" condition="begin with">C:\Windows\System32\</ImageLoaded>
        <ImageLoaded name="exclude=SysWOW64 DLL path" condition="begin with">C:\Windows\SysWOW64\</ImageLoaded>
        <ImageLoaded name="exclude=WinSxS DLL path" condition="begin with">C:\Windows\WinSxS\</ImageLoaded>
        <Signed name="exclude=signed DLL" condition="is">true</Signed>
      </ImageLoad>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!--
    ================================================================
    EVENT ID 8: CREATE REMOTE THREAD [CreateRemoteThread]
    Log all remote thread creation except core system processes.
    MITRE ATT&CK: T1055 (Process Injection)
    ================================================================
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="CreateRemoteThread" groupRelation="or">
      <CreateRemoteThread onmatch="exclude">
        <SourceImage name="exclude=csrss legitimate remote thread" condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\csrss.exe</SourceImage>
        <SourceImage name="exclude=wininit legitimate remote thread" condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\wininit.exe</SourceImage>
        <SourceImage name="exclude=winlogon legitimate remote thread" condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\winlogon.exe</SourceImage>
        <SourceImage name="exclude=services legitimate remote thread" condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\services.exe</SourceImage>
        <SourceImage name="exclude=lsass legitimate remote thread" condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\lsass.exe</SourceImage>
        <SourceImage name="exclude=svchost legitimate remote thread" condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\svchost.exe</SourceImage>
      </CreateRemoteThread>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!--
    ================================================================
    EVENT ID 9: RAW ACCESS READ [RawAccessRead]
    ENABLED on jump hosts - detects disk-level data access attempts.
    ================================================================
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="RawAccessRead" groupRelation="or">
      <RawAccessRead onmatch="exclude">
        <Image name="exclude=svchost raw read" condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\svchost.exe</Image>
      </RawAccessRead>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!--
      T1003.001 LSASS Memory
      Detect any process accessing lsass.exe. On a jump host, this should be rare.
      Investigate every match: check the source process, command line, and parent.
      Reference: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003/001/
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="ProcessAccess" groupRelation="or">
      <ProcessAccess onmatch="include">
        <TargetImage name="technique_id=T1003.001,technique=LSASS Memory,detection=Process accessing lsass.exe" condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\lsass.exe</TargetImage>
      </ProcessAccess>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!--
    ================================================================
    EVENT ID 11: FILE CREATED [FileCreate]
    Broad monitoring. Jump hosts should have very few file changes.
    ================================================================
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="FileCreate" groupRelation="or">
      <FileCreate onmatch="include">
        <!--
          T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder
          Files written to Startup locations are a classic persistence technique.
          On a jump host, no legitimate user should be writing here.
          Reference: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1547/001/
        -->
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1547.001,technique=Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder,detection=File created in Start Menu" condition="contains">\Start Menu</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1547.001,technique=Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder,detection=File created in Startup folder" condition="contains">\Startup</TargetFilename>
        <!--
          T1053.005 Scheduled Task
          Scheduled task XML files are dropped to enable persistence via schtasks/Task Scheduler.
          Reference: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1053/005/
        -->
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1053.005,technique=Scheduled Task,detection=Scheduled task XML created in Tasks directory" condition="contains">\Windows\System32\Tasks</TargetFilename>
        <!--
          Executable and script file types
          New scripts and binaries are unusual on a stable jump host. Each technique tag
          identifies the interpreter or execution mechanism the file targets.
          References:
            https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1204/002/
            https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/
        -->
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1204.002,technique=Malicious File,detection=Executable file created" condition="end with">.exe</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1574.002,technique=DLL Side-Loading,detection=DLL file created" condition="end with">.dll</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1543.003|T1014,technique=Windows Service,detection=System driver (.sys) file created" condition="end with">.sys</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1059.003,technique=Windows Command Shell,detection=Batch script created" condition="end with">.bat</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1059.003,technique=Windows Command Shell,detection=Command script created" condition="end with">.cmd</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1059.001,technique=PowerShell,detection=PowerShell script created" condition="end with">.ps1</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1059.005,technique=Visual Basic,detection=VBScript file created" condition="end with">.vbs</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1059.007,technique=JavaScript,detection=JavaScript file created" condition="end with">.js</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1059.005,technique=Visual Basic,detection=Windows Script File created" condition="end with">.wsf</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1218.005,technique=Mshta,detection=HTML Application file created" condition="end with">.hta</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1546.002,technique=Screensaver,detection=Screensaver executable created" condition="end with">.scr</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1218.007,technique=Msiexec,detection=MSI installer file created" condition="end with">.msi</TargetFilename>
        <!--
          T1560.001 Archive via Utility
          Archives on a jump host may indicate staging for exfiltration across network boundaries.
          Reference: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1560/001/
        -->
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1560.001,technique=Archive via Utility,detection=ZIP archive created" condition="end with">.zip</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1560.001,technique=Archive via Utility,detection=RAR archive created" condition="end with">.rar</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1560.001,technique=Archive via Utility,detection=7z archive created" condition="end with">.7z</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1204.002|T1027.006,technique=Malicious File,detection=ISO file created (common malware container)" condition="end with">.iso</TargetFilename>
        <!--
          T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer + T1074 Data Staged
          Files in user-accessible locations on a jump host warrant investigation.
          Jump hosts should not be used for general file storage.
          References:
            https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1105/
            https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1074/
        -->
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1105|T1074,technique=Ingress Tool Transfer,detection=File created in user Downloads folder on jump host" condition="contains">\Downloads\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1105|T1074,technique=Ingress Tool Transfer,detection=File created in user AppData Temp on jump host" condition="contains">\AppData\Local\Temp\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1105|T1074,technique=Ingress Tool Transfer,detection=File created in ProgramData folder" condition="contains">\ProgramData\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1105|T1074,technique=Ingress Tool Transfer,detection=File created on user Desktop on jump host" condition="contains">\Desktop\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1105|T1074,technique=Ingress Tool Transfer,detection=File created in user Documents on jump host" condition="contains">\Documents\</TargetFilename>
        <!--
          T1564.001 Hidden Files and Directories
          Recycle Bin is a common malware staging and hiding location.
          Reference: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564/001/
        -->
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1564.001,technique=Hidden Files and Directories,detection=File staged in Recycle Bin" condition="contains">\$Recycle.Bin\</TargetFilename>
        <!--
          T1484.001 Group Policy Modification
          Group Policy file changes affect system-wide configuration.
          Reference: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1484/001/
        -->
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1484.001,technique=Group Policy Modification,detection=Group Policy file modified" condition="contains">\GroupPolicy\</TargetFilename>
        <!--
          T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact
          Ransom note filename patterns. EKANS specifically targets ICS environments.
          Reference: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1486/
        -->
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1486,technique=Data Encrypted for Impact,detection=Ransom note filename containing DECRYPT" condition="contains">DECRYPT</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1486,technique=Data Encrypted for Impact,detection=Ransom note filename containing RANSOM" condition="contains">RANSOM</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1486,technique=Data Encrypted for Impact,detection=Ransom note filename containing RECOVER" condition="contains">RECOVER</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1486,technique=Data Encrypted for Impact,detection=EKANS ransomware note (Decrypt-Your-Files.txt)" condition="end with">Decrypt-Your-Files.txt</TargetFilename>
      </FileCreate>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!--
    ================================================================
    EVENT ID 12, 13, 14: REGISTRY EVENTS [RegistryEvent]
    Same as baselines plus AppLocker monitoring and Terminal Services keys.
    ================================================================
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="RegistryEvent" groupRelation="or">
      <RegistryEvent onmatch="include">
        <!--
          T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder
          Run/RunOnce registry keys are the most common Windows persistence locations.
          Reference: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1547/001/
        -->
        <TargetObject name="technique_id=T1547.001,technique=Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder,detection=Registry Run key modified" condition="contains">\CurrentVersion\Run</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject name="technique_id=T1547.001,technique=Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder,detection=Registry RunOnce key modified" condition="contains">\CurrentVersion\RunOnce</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject name="technique_id=T1547.001,technique=Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder,detection=RunServices key modified" condition="contains">\CurrentVersion\RunServices</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject name="technique_id=T1547.001,technique=Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder,detection=Explorer Policies Run key modified" condition="contains">\CurrentVersion\Policies\Explorer\Run</TargetObject>
        <!--
          T1543.003 Windows Service
          Service registry modifications can install or modify persistent services.
          Reference: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543/003/
        -->
        <TargetObject name="technique_id=T1543.003,technique=Windows Service,detection=Windows service registry key modified" condition="contains">\CurrentControlSet\Services</TargetObject>
        <!--
          T1053.005 Scheduled Task
          Reference: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1053/005/
        -->
        <TargetObject name="technique_id=T1053.005,technique=Scheduled Task,detection=Scheduled task cache modified" condition="contains">\Schedule\TaskCache</TargetObject>
        <!--
          T1546.010 AppInit DLLs
          Reference: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546/010/
        -->
        <TargetObject name="technique_id=T1546.010,technique=AppInit DLLs,detection=AppInit_DLLs registry value modified" condition="contains">\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Windows\AppInit_DLLs</TargetObject>
        <!--
          T1546.012 Image File Execution Options Injection
          IFEO can attach a debugger to any executable for persistence.
          Reference: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546/012/
        -->
        <TargetObject name="technique_id=T1546.012,technique=Image File Execution Options Injection,detection=IFEO registry key modified" condition="contains">\CurrentVersion\Image File Execution Options</TargetObject>
        <!--
          T1547.004 Winlogon Helper DLL
          Reference: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1547/004/
        -->
        <TargetObject name="technique_id=T1547.004,technique=Winlogon Helper DLL,detection=Winlogon Shell value modified" condition="contains">\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Winlogon\Shell</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject name="technique_id=T1547.004,technique=Winlogon Helper DLL,detection=Winlogon Userinit value modified" condition="contains">\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Winlogon\Userinit</TargetObject>
        <!--
          T1562.001 Disable or Modify Tools
          AppLocker policy changes may indicate an attacker bypassing application allowlisting.
          Reference: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1562/001/
        -->
        <TargetObject name="technique_id=T1562.001,technique=Disable or Modify Tools,detection=AppLocker policy modified" condition="contains">\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\SrpV2</TargetObject>
        <!--
          T1021.001 Remote Desktop Protocol
          Terminal Services configuration changes affect RDP behavior, the primary
          jump host access mechanism. Investigate any changes.
          Reference: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/001/
        -->
        <TargetObject name="technique_id=T1021.001,technique=Remote Desktop Protocol,detection=Terminal Services configuration modified" condition="contains">\Terminal Services</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject name="technique_id=T1021.001,technique=Remote Desktop Protocol,detection=Terminal Server WinStations modified" condition="contains">\Terminal Server\WinStations</TargetObject>
        <!--
          T1490 Inhibit System Recovery + T1112 Modify Registry + T1562.001 Disable or Modify Tools
          Ransomware-relevant registry changes.
          References:
            https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1490/
            https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003/001/
            https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1562/001/
        -->
        <TargetObject name="technique_id=T1490,technique=Inhibit System Recovery,detection=SafeBoot configuration modified" condition="contains">\Control\SafeBoot</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject name="technique_id=T1112|T1003.001,technique=Modify Registry,detection=WDigest credential caching enabled" condition="contains">\SecurityProviders\WDigest</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject name="technique_id=T1562.001,technique=Disable or Modify Tools,detection=Windows Defender policy modified" condition="contains">\Policies\Microsoft\Windows Defender</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject name="technique_id=T1562.001,technique=Disable or Modify Tools,detection=Sysmon Autologger registry tampering" condition="contains">\Autologger\EventLog-Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon</TargetObject>
      </RegistryEvent>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!--
    ================================================================
    EVENT ID 15: FILE CREATE STREAM HASH [FileCreateStreamHash]
    Detects alternate data streams (Mark of the Web, hidden data).
    ================================================================
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="FileCreateStreamHash" groupRelation="or">
      <FileCreateStreamHash onmatch="include">
        <!--
          T1564.004 NTFS File Attributes + T1553.005 Mark of the Web Bypass
          Alternate data streams on executables and scripts indicate downloaded content
          and potential MOTW bypass attempts.
          References:
            https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564/004/
            https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1553/005/
        -->
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1564.004|T1553.005,technique=NTFS File Attributes,detection=Executable Mark of the Web stream created" condition="end with">.exe</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1564.004|T1553.005,technique=NTFS File Attributes,detection=DLL Mark of the Web stream created" condition="end with">.dll</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1564.004|T1553.005,technique=NTFS File Attributes,detection=Batch script Mark of the Web stream created" condition="end with">.bat</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1564.004|T1553.005,technique=NTFS File Attributes,detection=Command script Mark of the Web stream created" condition="end with">.cmd</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1564.004|T1553.005,technique=NTFS File Attributes,detection=PowerShell script Mark of the Web stream created" condition="end with">.ps1</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1564.004|T1553.005,technique=NTFS File Attributes,detection=VBScript Mark of the Web stream created" condition="end with">.vbs</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1564.004|T1553.005,technique=NTFS File Attributes,detection=JavaScript Mark of the Web stream created" condition="end with">.js</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1564.004|T1553.005,technique=NTFS File Attributes,detection=HTA Mark of the Web stream created" condition="end with">.hta</TargetFilename>
      </FileCreateStreamHash>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!--
    ================================================================
    EVENT ID 17, 18: NAMED PIPE EVENTS [PipeEvent]
    Log ALL named pipes. Jump hosts should have minimal pipe activity.
    ================================================================
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="PipeCreated" groupRelation="or">
      <PipeEvent onmatch="exclude"/>
    </RuleGroup>
    <!--
    NOTE: C2 framework pipes (Cobalt Strike MSSE-*, postex_*, PsExec PSEXESVC,
    etc.) are caught by the log-all approach. See other configs for named
    C2 pipe detection rules if you switch to exclude-based filtering.
    -->

    <!--
    ================================================================
    EVENT ID 19, 20, 21: WMI EVENTS [WmiEvent]
    Log all WMI activity.
    MITRE ATT&CK: T1546.003 (Event Triggered Execution: WMI)
    ================================================================
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="WmiEvent" groupRelation="or">
      <WmiEvent onmatch="exclude"/>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!--
    ================================================================
    EVENT ID 22: DNS QUERY [DnsQuery]
    Log ALL DNS queries. Jump hosts should have minimal DNS activity.
    Any unexpected domain resolution warrants investigation.
    ================================================================
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="DnsQuery" groupRelation="or">
      <DnsQuery onmatch="exclude">
        <QueryName name="exclude=Microsoft DNS query" condition="end with">.microsoft.com</QueryName>
        <QueryName name="exclude=Windows Update DNS query" condition="end with">.windowsupdate.com</QueryName>
      </DnsQuery>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!--
    ================================================================
    EVENT ID 23: FILE DELETE (ARCHIVED) [FileDelete]
    Disabled - use Event ID 26 (FileDeleteDetected) instead for lower overhead.
    ================================================================
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="FileDelete" groupRelation="or">
      <FileDelete onmatch="include"/>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!--
    ================================================================
    EVENT ID 24: CLIPBOARD CHANGE [ClipboardChange]
    ENABLED on jump hosts. Detects data transfer via clipboard between
    RDP sessions. Important for detecting data exfiltration across
    network boundaries (SANS Control #2, #4).
    MITRE ATT&CK: T1115 (Clipboard Data)
    NOTE: This generates events every time clipboard content changes.
    On a jump host this is acceptable - clipboard transfers between
    networks are exactly what needs monitoring.
    ================================================================
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="ClipboardChange" groupRelation="or">
      <ClipboardChange onmatch="exclude"/>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!--
    ================================================================
    EVENT ID 25: PROCESS TAMPERING [ProcessTampering]
    Log all process tampering.
    MITRE ATT&CK: T1055.012 (Process Hollowing)
    ================================================================
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="ProcessTampering" groupRelation="or">
      <ProcessTampering onmatch="exclude"/>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!--
    ================================================================
    EVENT ID 26: FILE DELETE DETECTED [FileDeleteDetected]
    Broader monitoring on jump hosts - any file deletion is notable.
    ================================================================
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="FileDeleteDetected" groupRelation="or">
      <FileDeleteDetected onmatch="include">
        <!--
          T1070.004 File Deletion
          Deletion of executables and scripts on a jump host may indicate evidence
          cleanup after lateral movement or execution.
          Reference: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1070/004/
        -->
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1070.004,technique=File Deletion,detection=Executable file deleted" condition="end with">.exe</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1070.004,technique=File Deletion,detection=DLL file deleted" condition="end with">.dll</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1070.004,technique=File Deletion,detection=System driver deleted" condition="end with">.sys</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1070.004,technique=File Deletion,detection=PowerShell script deleted" condition="end with">.ps1</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1070.004,technique=File Deletion,detection=Batch script deleted" condition="end with">.bat</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1070.004,technique=File Deletion,detection=Command script deleted" condition="end with">.cmd</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1070.004,technique=File Deletion,detection=VBScript file deleted" condition="end with">.vbs</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1070.004,technique=File Deletion,detection=JavaScript file deleted" condition="end with">.js</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1070.004,technique=File Deletion,detection=ZIP archive deleted" condition="end with">.zip</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1070.004,technique=File Deletion,detection=RAR archive deleted" condition="end with">.rar</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1070.004,technique=File Deletion,detection=7z archive deleted" condition="end with">.7z</TargetFilename>
        <!--
          T1490 Inhibit System Recovery + T1485 Data Destruction
          Backup and database file deletion is a strong ransomware indicator.
          References:
            https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1490/
            https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1485/
        -->
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1490|T1485,technique=Inhibit System Recovery,detection=Backup file (.bak) deletion" condition="end with">.bak</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1490|T1485,technique=Inhibit System Recovery,detection=Virtual hard disk (.vhd) deletion" condition="end with">.vhd</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1490|T1485,technique=Inhibit System Recovery,detection=Virtual hard disk (.vhdx) deletion" condition="end with">.vhdx</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1490|T1485,technique=Inhibit System Recovery,detection=SQL Server data file (.mdf) deletion" condition="end with">.mdf</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename name="technique_id=T1490|T1485,technique=Inhibit System Recovery,detection=SQL Server log file (.ldf) deletion" condition="end with">.ldf</TargetFilename>
      </FileDeleteDetected>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!--
    ================================================================
    EVENT ID 29: FILE EXECUTABLE DETECTED [FileExecutableDetected]
    Schema 4.90 feature. Detects new executable files (PE format)
    written to disk. Critical on jump hosts - any new executable
    warrants immediate investigation.
    ================================================================
    -->
    <RuleGroup name="FileExecutableDetected" groupRelation="or">
      <FileExecutableDetected onmatch="exclude"/>
    </RuleGroup>

  </EventFiltering>
</Sysmon>
